{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"grep -n 'VerifyLeader\\\\|Nonvoter' ../agent-source/raft_test.go | head -45; grep -n '\"'^func'\"\\\\|type cluster' ../agent-source/testing.go | head -70; cat ../agent-source/docs/apply.md; cat /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/resources/skills/evidence-review/references/experiments.md\"","aggregated_output":"40:\t// TODO (slackpad) - Barrier, VerifyLeader, and GetConfiguration can get\n46:\tif f := raft.AddNonvoter(ServerID(\"id\"), ServerAddress(\"addr\"), 0, 0); f.Error() != ErrRaftShutdown {\n118:\t\t\tserver.Suffrage = Nonvoter\n578:\t\t\tleader.VerifyLeader()\n1866:func TestRaft_VerifyLeader(t *testing.T) {\n1875:\tverify := leader.VerifyLeader()\n1883:func TestRaft_VerifyLeader_Single(t *testing.T) {\n1892:\tverify := leader.VerifyLeader()\n1900:func TestRaft_VerifyLeader_Fail(t *testing.T) {\n1928:\tverify := leader.VerifyLeader()\n1940:func TestRaft_VerifyLeader_PartialConnect(t *testing.T) {\n1966:\tverify := leader.VerifyLeader()\n2254:\tfuture := c.Leader().AddNonvoter(c1.rafts[0].localID, c1.rafts[0].localAddr, 0, 1*time.Second)\n2582:\t\tl.AddNonvoter(ServerID(\"\"), ServerAddress(\"\"), 0, 0),\n2636:func TestRaft_LeadershipTransferIgnoresNonvoters(t *testing.T) {\n3008:\tfuture = leader.AddNonvoter(followerRemoved.localID, followerRemoved.localAddr, 0, 0)\n3155:\t\t{\"NonVoter\", fields{conf: DefaultConfig(), servers: []Server{{Nonvoter, \"first\", \"\"}}, serverID: \"first\"}, Follower},\n24:func inmemConfig(t testing.TB) *Config {\n54:func getMockFSM(fsm FSM) *MockFSM {\n76:func (m *MockFSM) Apply(log *Log) interface{} {\n84:func (m *MockFSM) Snapshot() (FSMSnapshot, error) {\n91:func (m *MockFSM) Restore(inp io.ReadCloser) error {\n103:func (m *MockFSM) Logs() [][]byte {\n110:func (m *MockFSMConfigStore) StoreConfiguration(index uint64, config Configuration) {\n118:func (m *MockSnapshot) Persist(sink SnapshotSink) error {\n130:func (m *MockSnapshot) Release() {\n140:func (m *MockMonotonicLogStore) IsMonotonic() bool {\n145:func (m *MockMonotonicLogStore) FirstIndex() (uint64, error) {\n150:func (m *MockMonotonicLogStore) LastIndex() (uint64, error) {\n155:func (m *MockMonotonicLogStore) GetLog(index uint64, log *Log) error {\n160:func (m *MockMonotonicLogStore) StoreLog(log *Log) error {\n165:func (m *MockMonotonicLogStore) StoreLogs(logs []*Log) error {\n170:func (m *MockMonotonicLogStore) DeleteRange(min uint64, max uint64) error {\n182:func (a *testLoggerAdapter) Write(d []byte) (int, error) {\n196:func newTestLogger(tb testing.TB) hclog.Logger {\n212:func newTestLoggerWithPrefix(tb testing.TB, prefix string) hclog.Logger {\n223:type cluster struct {\n243:func (c *cluster) Merge(other *cluster) {\n252:func (c *cluster) RemoveServer(id ServerID) {\n264:func (c *cluster) notifyFailed() {\n280:func (c *cluster) Failf(format string, args ...interface{}) {\n291:func (c *cluster) FailNowf(format string, args ...interface{}) {\n297:func (c *cluster) Close() {\n326:func (c *cluster) WaitEventChan(ctx context.Context, filter FilterFn) <-chan struct{} {\n348:func (c *cluster) WaitEvent(filter FilterFn, timeout time.Duration) {\n361:func (c *cluster) WaitForReplication(fsmLength int) {\n394:func (c *cluster) pollState(s RaftState) ([]*Raft, uint64) {\n411:func (c *cluster) GetInState(s RaftState) []*Raft {\n491:func (c *cluster) Leader() *Raft {\n502:func (c *cluster) Followers() []*Raft {\n509:func (c *cluster) WaitForFollowers(expFollowers int) []*Raft {\n518:func (c *cluster) FullyConnect() {\n531:func (c *cluster) Disconnect(a ServerAddress) {\n544:func (c *cluster) Partition(far []ServerAddress) {\n578:func (c *cluster) IndexOf(r *Raft) int {\n589:func (c *cluster) EnsureLeader(t *testing.T, expect ServerAddress) {\n614:func (c *cluster) EnsureSame(t *testing.T) {\n681:func (c *cluster) getConfiguration(r *Raft) Configuration {\n692:func (c *cluster) EnsureSamePeers(t *testing.T) {\n733:func makeCluster(t *testing.T, opts *MakeClusterOpts) *cluster {\n839:func MakeCluster(n int, t *testing.T, conf *Config) *cluster {\n848:func MakeClusterNoBootstrap(n int, t *testing.T, conf *Config) *cluster {\n856:func MakeClusterCustom(t *testing.T, opts *MakeClusterOpts) *cluster {\n861:func FileSnapTest(t *testing.T) (string, *FileSnapshotStore) {\n# Raft Apply\n\nApply is the primary operation provided by raft. A client calls `raft.Apply` to apply\na command to the FSM. A command will first be committed, i.e., durably stored on a\nquorum of raft nodes. Then, the committed command is applied to fsm.\n\nThis sequence diagram shows the steps involved in a `raft.Apply` operation. Each box\nacross the top is a separate thread. The name in the box identifies the state of the peer\n(leader or follower) and the thread (`<peer state>:<thread name>`). When there are\nmultiple copies of the thread, it is indicated with `(each peer)`.\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n   autonumber\n \n   participant client\n   participant leadermain as leader:main\n   participant leaderfsm as leader:fsm\n   participant leaderreplicate as leader:replicate (each peer)\n   participant followermain as follower:main (each peer)\n   participant followerfsm as follower:fsm (each peer)\n \n   client-)leadermain: applyCh to dispatchLogs\n   leadermain->>leadermain: store logs to disk\n \n   leadermain-)leaderreplicate: triggerCh\n   leaderreplicate-->>followermain: Transport.AppendEntries RPC\n \n   followermain->>followermain: store logs to disk\n \n   opt leader commit index is ahead of peer commit index\n       followermain-)followerfsm: fsmMutateCh <br>apply committed logs\n       followerfsm->>followerfsm: fsm.Apply\n   end\n \n   followermain-->>leaderreplicate: respond success=true\n   leaderreplicate->>leaderreplicate: update commitment\n \n   opt quorum commit index has increased\n       leaderreplicate-)leadermain: commitCh\n       leadermain-)leaderfsm: fsmMutateCh\n       leaderfsm->>leaderfsm: fsm.Apply\n       leaderfsm-)client: future.respond\n   end\n\n```\n\nFollowing is the description of each step as shown in the above diagram\n\n1. The raft node handles the `raft.Apply` call by creating a new log entry and send the entry\nto the `applyCh` channel.\n\n2. If the node is not a leader, the method will return an error of `ErrNotLeader`. Otherwise,\nthe main loop of the leader node calls `raft.dispatchLogs` to write the log entry locally.\n\n3. `raft.dispatchLogs` also sends a notification to the `f.triggerCh` of each follower (`map[ServerID]*followerReplication`) to start replicating log entries to the followers.\n\n4. For each follower, the leader has started a long running routine (`replicate`) to\nreplicates log entries. On receiving a log entry to the `triggerCh`, the `replicate`\nroutine makes the `Transport.AppendEntries` RPC call to do the replication. The log entries\nto be replicated are from the follower's nextIndex to min(nextIndex + maxAppendEntries, \nleader's lastIndex). Another parameter to AppendEntries is the LeaderCommitIndex. Following\nis some examples:\n\n```\nAppendEntries(Log: 1..5, LeaderCommitIndex: 0)   // Replicating log entries 1..5, \n                                                 // the leader hasn't committed any log entry;\nAppendEntries(Log: 6..8, LeaderCommitIndex: 4)   // Replicating log entries 6..8,\n                                                 // log 0..4 are committed after the leader receives\n                                                 // a quorum of responses\nAppendEntries(Log: 9, LeaderCommitIndex: 8)      // Replicating log entry 9,\n                                                 // log 5..8 are committed.\nAppendEntries(Log: , LeaderCommitIndex: 9)       // no new log, bumping the commit index\n                                                 // to let the follower stay up to date of the\n                                                 // latest committed entries\n```\n\n5. The follower which receives the `appendEntries` RPC calls invokes `raft.appendEntries` to handle\nthe request. It appends any new entries to the local log store.\n\n6. In the same method on the follower as step 5, if the LeaderCommitIndex > this follower's\ncommitIndex, the follower updates it's commitIndex to min(LeaderCommitIndex, index of its last\nlog entries). In the first `AppendEntries` call of the above example, the follower won't\nupdate it's commitIndex, because LeaderCommitIndex is 0. The last RPC call doesn't contain\nany new log, whereas the follower will update its commitIndex to 9.\n\nFurther, the follower start `processLogs` to send all the committed entries that haven't been\napplied to fsm (`fsmMutateCh <- batch`). Otherwise (i.e., `commitIndex <= lastApplied`),\nthe appendEntries RPC call returns success.\n\nTherefore, it's possible that a very small window of time exists when all followers have\ncommitted the log to disk, the write has been realized in the FSM of the leader but the\nfollowers have not yet applied the log to their FSM.\n\n7. The peer applies the committed entries to the FSM.\n\n8. If all went well, the follower responds success (`resp.Success = true`) to the \n`appendEntries` RPC call.\n\n9. On receiving the successful response from `Transport.AppendEntries`, the leader needs to\nupdate the fsm based on the replicated log entries. Specifically, the leader finds the\nhighest log entry index that has been replicated to a quorum of the servers (\n`if quorumMatchIndex > c.commitIndex`), update `commitIndex` to that index, and\nnotify through the `commitCh` channel.\n\n10. The leader receives the notification on the  `r.leaderState.commitCh` channel and starts\ngrouping the entries that can be applied to the fsm.\n\n11. `processLogs` applies all the committed entries that haven't been applied by batching the log entries and forwarding them through the `fsmMutateCh` channel to fsm.\n\n12. The actual place applying the committed log entries is in the main loop of `runFSM()`.\n\n13. After the log entries that contains the client req are applied to the fsm, the fsm\nmodule will set the responses to the client request (`req.future.respond(nil)`). From the\nclient's point of view, the future returned by `raft.Apply` should now be unblocked and\ncalls to `Error()` or `Response()` should return the data at this point.\nEXECUTION Observe actual implementation effects under explicit inputs, caller policy and authorized isolation. Contract applicability may remain unresolved during exploration; execution permission may not. A changed value or tests_passed is not a violation oracle. Exploration remains construction knowledge: a later requirement needs a separately fixed check, fresh execution and correspondence review, not retroactive relabelling. Missing capability, compilation failure, timeout, panic and a completed property comparison remain distinct. Retain failures; never precompute expected observations or alter protocol behavior to create a finding.\n\nCOMPLETION Separate the proposition's observation endpoint, necessary caller completion, and resource cleanup. A normal nonzero test exit may leave a completed violating witness intact only through checker_correspondence.execution_attribution binding the exact target CheckRun, checker and valid event indices, retained failure stream lines and fixed harness lines. Use source_ids and rationale to justify that all duties needed by that witness completed before an independent failure, with no corruption of history, identity or values. Machine validation checks these references and eligibility, not causal independence; stdout/stderr line numbers are not a common clock. Ordinary no_issue_found never authorizes nonzero exits. Preserve raw failure, other scenario debts and later challenges; no holds-only, timeout, cancellation, signal, build failure, missing premise, ambiguous or contradictory observation can use this path. Do not remove required join/close, ignore exceptions or kill workers after printing to manufacture PASS; unsupported short wall-clock limits are not extra properties. Changed harnesses, premises, predicates or scope still need the existing new-artifact and actual-execution path. Initialization, driver compliance and observation validity belong in prerequisites, controls or diagnostics, not duplicate properties. Check a wait's return or record actual attainment when it establishes a necessary premise; when waiting is the investigated behavior, requiring success would discard the failure history. Sleep establishes elapsed time, not synchronization, completion or a coherent snapshot; a printed readiness flag is not a prerequisite witness. A diagnostic control need not satisfy the principal scenario's premises or acquire a separate obligation: select formal applicability from independently declared input/policy fields before execution, retaining control output. Distinct formal scenarios need their actual admission and premises; incompatible histories may need separate checks. Never select scenarios by observed results or missing premises after execution. Role readiness does not imply that earlier work drained. Across preparation, policy changes and delivery, preserve caller/transport duties; prefer one policy-selected delivery path and record the identity, provenance and order needed for attribution. Distinguish receipt, per-message-type queueing and dispatch; preserve the replaced layer's ordering without assuming global FIFO or arbitrary reordering. Legal in-flight work need not be emptied. End controlled work independently of the forbidden behavior. Observe admission, completion and final state for the same operation before treating absence as bounded holds. Missing completion, identity, result events and tool timeout remain unknown. A finite window records non-completion, not a deadline violation or infinite stalling. A repeatable suffix must account for behavior-determining state, schedule, queues, timers and callbacks; equal business fields do not close execution. A rescue stimulus may itself be required caller work.\n\nCORRELATION Monitor.admission_alias selects the declared prerequisite admitting operations whose completion is owed, not the first array entry. Harness.prerequisites contains prerequisites only; monitors select independent results. During construction, compare ObservableProperty.identity_fields with the actual prerequisite and result events together. Shared setup may support multiple operations through explicit plan relationships; neither duplicate identical events mechanically nor rely on controller-supplied identity. Do not repeat results as prerequisites or each identity equality as a Comparison. References declare predecessor relationships; unrelated prerequisites have no implicit order. Assertions may use any declared alias actually preceding the result. Missing identity/reference fields or nonunique associations remain unknown. Independent applicability precedes unrelated history requirements; alias-dependent applicability follows correlation. Match operations separately. A complete violating witness survives missing results in demonstrably independent operations with partial coverage; same-witness ambiguity or potentially global parse loss blocks attribution. Process success, observed differences and complete correlation are separate facts. Associate input and output by independent identity, not compared values or a context expected to change. Unsupported temporal expressions remain an explicit limitation.\n\nMEANING Distinguish the written obligation, the proposition encoded by applicability/trigger/antecedent/assertion, and what this execution actually compared. Type, count or numeric-position equality may be necessary without establishing content or identity equality. Record comparisons and scope limits separately. Identify live object, copy, cache or caller-maintained observations, their writers and the synchronization or ownership boundary supporting the read; multiple fields must represent the state the proposition needs. Include ownership of injection flags, counters and sampling itself. An export discrepancy need not mean active protocol state changed. Match observation to the claim: stopping may change the behavior; neither shutdown sampling, locks, public APIs, dual reads nor race detection are universally required. Challenge specific unreliable reads, not all concurrent checks. Keep unresolved contracts, counterevidence, input legality and observation differences explicit; CheckRun owns execution status. An implementation/requirement discrepancy is the subject, not a conflict between requirement sources. Real contradictions remain conflicts.\n\nCONFIRMATION Reconstruct decisive relationships from the fixed test, actual constructors/producers and raw events; the plan's legality account is a claim to verify. Check prepared state, substituted calls and identity/context/generation across stages; constructible inputs or isolated fragments do not establish one legal history. Preserve relevant queues, callbacks, lock ownership and caller/transport duties. For waiting, missing replies or retained resources, trace waiter, result/cancel/close producer, normal shutdown entry and the observation's shutdown phase. Verify which owner/caller actually performs cleanup: no local timeout does not prove permanent waiting, and an available Close method does not prove it ran. A direct post-stop handler call differs from production dispatch still reaching it. Sourced local interface or exit duties need no whole-deployment proof. Review oracle, parsing, reached premises and completion together. Use rationale, source_ids, counterevidence, challenged_components and exact ReviewIssues for discrepancies; choose driver repair, encoding or F2/F3 by what changes, never weaken the written responsibility to rescue confirmation. Incompatible inputs are attribution gaps. Local functions, injectable storage, bounded initialization and substitute schedules remain valid when the sourced proposition permits them. Preserve measurements while correspondence is pending; complete local counterexamples survive independent wider-history or endpoint limits. New source counterevidence may challenge prior confirmation; an unobserved wider endpoint remains a limit.\nNECESSITY For S implies E, compare S when independently established E is false, or use equivalent supported implication encoding; never strengthen to S equals E or filter the result through applicability. In a complete implication comparison, observed S=false retains holds but supports only that S did not occur here, not E or universal consistency; S=true actually tests E. Missing results, identity or prerequisites are not observed falsity and cannot supply holds. Do not require every conditional branch to execute before confirming a complete local counterexample, or infer eventual success from a permitted pending control.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
